    example: "Two 10 kΩ resistors divide 5 V: Vout = 5 × 10/(10 + 10) = 2.5 V, wasting 5 V / 20 kΩ = 0.25 mA. The Thévenin resistance is 10 kΩ ∥ 10 kΩ = 5 kΩ. Now connect a 10 kΩ load: Vout = 2.5 V × 10/(5 + 10) ≈ 1.67 V, a third lower than expected. With a 1 MΩ load instead, Vout = 2.5 × 1000/1005 ≈ 2.49 V, practically unchanged. Rule of thumb: keep the load at least ten times Rth for under 10 % error, or a hundred times for under 1 %. To measure a 12 V battery with a 3.3 V ADC, 100 kΩ over 33 kΩ gives 12 × 33/133 ≈ 2.98 V.",
