    aliases: ["ac", "reactance", "impedance", "phase", "sine wave", "rms", "complex impedance", "frequency", "capacitive reactance", "inductive reactance"],
    summary: "Capacitors and inductors act like resistors whose value depends on frequency, and they shift the timing of current relative to voltage. Impedance bundles resistance and this frequency-dependent reactance into one number.",
    sections: [
      { h: "Alternating current", p: "Direct current (DC) flows one way at a steady level. Alternating current (AC) swings back and forth, usually as a smooth sine wave. Its frequency f, in hertz (Hz), counts complete cycles per second; mains is 50 or 60 Hz, audio spans 20 Hz to 20 kHz, and Wi-Fi runs at 2.4 GHz. The amplitude is the peak voltage. Because the voltage spends much of its time below the peak, we often quote the RMS (root mean square) value, the DC voltage that would heat a resistor equally: for a sine wave, Vrms = Vpeak / √2 ≈ 0.707 × Vpeak. Any real signal, from music to a digital edge, can be treated as a sum of sine waves of different frequencies." },
      { h: "Reactance: resistance that depends on frequency", p: "A capacitor blocks DC completely once charged, but an AC signal keeps charging and discharging it, so current flows back and forth. The faster the swings, the less time charge has to build up opposing voltage, so the capacitor passes high frequencies easily. Its opposition, called capacitive reactance, is X_C = 1/(2πfC), large at low frequency and small at high frequency. An inductor is the opposite: it resists changes in current, so the faster things change the harder it fights. Its inductive reactance is X_L = 2πfL, zero at DC and growing with frequency. Reactance is measured in ohms, and it is what the app's Reactance calculator computes." },
      { h: "Phase: when current and voltage disagree", p: "In a resistor, current and voltage rise and fall together. In a capacitor, current flows most while voltage is changing fastest, so the current peaks a quarter-cycle (90°) before the voltage; we say current leads. In an inductor current lags voltage by 90°. A useful picture is a child on a swing: the push (force) is greatest at the ends, while speed is greatest in the middle, a quarter-cycle apart. Because of this timing difference, a capacitor or ideal inductor stores energy and hands it back each cycle instead of turning it into heat. That is the key difference from resistance, which only dissipates." },
      { h: "Impedance in plain words", p: "Impedance (Z, in ohms) is the total opposition to AC, combining resistance and reactance. Because their effects are a quarter-cycle apart, they do not add like ordinary numbers; they add like the two sides of a right-angled triangle. The size of the impedance is |Z| = √(R² + X²), and the phase angle between voltage and current is φ = arctan(X / R). Engineers write this as a complex number, Z = R + jX, where the j (the imaginary unit) is just a label saying 'this part is 90° out of step'. You do not need complex arithmetic to use the idea: think of a length and an angle. The app's AC analysis does the complex maths at every frequency to draw its Bode plots." },
    ],
