    example: "A 100 nF capacitor has X_C = 1/(2π × 50 × 100 × 10⁻⁹) ≈ 31.8 kΩ at 50 Hz, about 1.59 kΩ at 1 kHz, and just 1.59 Ω at 1 MHz: nearly a short circuit for radio-frequency noise, which is why it works as a decoupling capacitor. Put it in series with a 1 kΩ resistor and drive with 1.59 kHz, where X_C ≈ 1 kΩ. The impedance is √(1000² + 1000²) ≈ 1.41 kΩ, not 2 kΩ, and current leads voltage by 45°. A 10 µH inductor at the same 1.59 kHz has X_L = 2π × 1590 × 10⁻⁵ ≈ 0.1 Ω, almost nothing, but at 100 MHz it reaches 6.3 kΩ.",
