    example: "A low-pass filter for a temperature sensor uses R = 1.6 kΩ and C = 100 nF. f_c = 1/(2π × 1600 × 100 × 10⁻⁹) ≈ 995 Hz, call it 1 kHz. At 100 Hz the gain is 1/√(1 + 0.01) ≈ 0.995, a loss of just 0.04 dB. At 10 kHz it is 1/√(1 + 100) ≈ 0.0995, about −20 dB, so 1 V of noise becomes 0.1 V. At 100 kHz it is about −40 dB, or 10 mV. To get −40 dB already at 10 kHz you would need a second-order filter with the same 1 kHz cut-off, for example a Sallen-Key Butterworth stage designed with the app's Filters calculator.",
    pitfalls: [
      "Ignoring source and load impedance: the next stage's input resistance changes both the gain and the cut-off of a passive filter.",
      "Expecting a brick wall; at the cut-off the signal is already 3 dB down, and just above it little is removed.",
      "Cascading two identical RC sections and expecting the same cut-off; the combined −3 dB point moves lower.",
      "Using wide-tolerance capacitors and being surprised the cut-off is 20 % off.",
    ],
    calculators: ["filter", "react", "opamp", "sensor"],
    related: ["theory-reactance-impedance", "theory-decibels", "theory-resonance", "theory-opamps", "theory-adc-sampling", "capacitor"],
